_answer` (no computer attached: it did not answer in time, or the user cancelled the request while it waited; nothing is left pending). A session is attached to at most one folder at a time, and it stays tied to the first computer it attaches to: to use a different computer, the user starts a new session. Asking again for the folder the session is attached to reconnects it: that attachment ends first, then the computer is asked afresh. While this session is attached to a folder (list_computer_folders shows it as `current`), do not call this tool for a different folder, and while an earlier request is still waiting, do not call it at all: the user would be shown the permission prompt, and the request is refused even if they allow it. If the user wants a different folder, tell them which computer and folder this session is connected to, and that they can disconnect it (how is below) and then ask you again. If the request is refused or the computer does not answer, do not re-ask in chat; carry on without it and say what remains blocked, including what the refusal says. You have no tool that disconnects the computer: only the user can. If they ask how: from this conversation's menu or its connected-computer indicator on claude.ai; or on that computer, wherever they set up Remote Control there (in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control; if the folder is served by the `claude remote-control` command, by stopping that command, which disconnects every session using the folder). The desktop app's settings page is also where they choose which folders are in the list.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder", "schema": {"description": "Connect THIS session to a folder the user's own computer serves through Remote Control, so this session's commands and file tools can run there. Calling this tool IS the request, and a person answers it every time (no permission mode and no stored rule does). So do NOT ask in chat first; once the task n